{"swagger": "2.0", "info": {"title": "BPstat Data API", "description": "The _BPstat Data API_ provides programmatic open access to the extensive set of statistical data and metadata made available through Bank of Portugal's [statistics portal](https://bpstat.bportugal.pt/), in a way that intends to facilitate its use in existing analysis tools as well as custom-made scripts and applications.\n\n\n# Basic Concepts\n\nThe core purpose of the _BPstat Data API_ is to provide access to statistical observations (values over time). In order to achieve this purpose effectively, it's important to start by briefly describing how observations are organized, the response format used to represent them, and how the two are related. If you're already familiar with these concepts, jump directly to a **[practical example](#section/Getting-Started)**.\n\n## Statistical Entities\n\nStarting from the bottom up, **observations** are values over time which are grouped into **series**, where a series is a _unique_ set of _specific_ characteristics that describe the observations. These characteristics include things such as the metric used, the value's unit of measure, the interval (recurrence) at which the observation is taken, the geographical region it refers to, among several others. This means two series that, for example, differ just in their recurrence (although closely related) are **different series** under this model.\n\n> Some series represent aggregates of other, more granular, series. For example, prefer fetching an annual series (when available) instead of adding observations from monthly series yourself.\n\nSeries characteristics are referred throughout this documentation as **categories** and related categories are grouped together into **dimensions** (for this reason categories can also be called _dimension members_). One example of a category is \"Portugal\" which, along with other countries, could be grouped under a \"Territory\" dimension.\n\n> The **reference date** is a special characteristic: it isn't part of the series but of the observation itself. Remembering this subtle distinction is important to avoid confusion as the reference date is often used in some of the same contexts where dimensions are used (as just another dimension) to simplify API calls and data representation. Nevertheless, you will never find it in the set of dimensions of any series, or even in the set of dimensions of any statistical domain.\n\nSeries described by **categories** from the same set of **dimensions** are grouped together in **datasets**. The series in a given dataset usually share the same overall topic (or theme) but that isn't mandatory: datasets group series by their _structure_, not necessarily by their _meaning_.\n\nTopics have their own representation as a tree of statistical **domains**. Each domain contains every dataset holding at least one series related to the domain's topic. This means the same dataset can appear in multiple domains but returns only a subset of its series when seen from each of them.\n\n> Some domains merely serve as more general umbrellas for other sub-domains and contain no datasets themselves.\n\nBefore going further, it's important to note that the model just described is slightly different from the model used throughout [BPstat](https://bpstat.bportugal.pt/). In particular, the concept of _datasets_ as groups of closely related series is specific to the _BPstat Data API_. It is also a simpler model, as hierarchical relationships for categories and dimensions aren't (explicitly) exposed at this time.\n\n\n## The JSON-stat Format\n\nThe concept of **dataset** as the way to group related series arises from the choice of format in which observation data is returned by the API. HTTP responses containing observations follow the [JSON-stat](https://json-stat.org/) format, more specifically [JSON-stat v2.0](https://json-stat.org/format/), which represents them in [tabular form](https://json-stat.org/format/#value).\n\n\n> If you use a programming language or tool for which a client library is [available](https://json-stat.org/tools/), a close understanding of the JSON-stat format may not be necessary as some of these libraries integrate closely with popular environments on their respective platforms (for example, the [pyjstat](https://pypi.org/project/pyjstat/) library returns regular [pandas](https://pandas.pydata.org/) dataframes). In either case, we recommend at least a basic understanding of its principles.\n\nSome of the statistical entities described in the [previous section](#section/Basic-Concepts/Statistical-Entities) cannot be properly described using JSON-stat alone. For example, series categories are represented as a dictionary inside an [extension](https://json-stat.org/format/#extension) attribute in a dataset, and this extension mechanism is also used in JSON-stat collections to encapsulate entity IDs when necessary.\n\nAPI endpoints that return metadata which can't be represented in JSON-stat use a simpler JSON format. Please refer to each endpoint's documentation section for more information.\n\n\n# Getting Started\n\nThe following example illustrates a simple use-case and serves as a starting point to understand the general workings of the _BPstat Data API_. Although the [Python](https://www.python.org/) programming language is used, this is not intended as a tutorial for Python or any of the used libraries/frameworks. Please refer to their respective documentation if you wish to explore them further.\n\n## Usage Example\n\nIn this example, we're going to use [Jupyter notebook](https://jupyter.org/) to plot a GDP-related series, which will require a few libraries: the [pyjstat](https://pypi.org/project/pyjstat/) library will be used to read the [JSON-stat](https://json-stat.org/) data and [requests](https://pypi.org/project/requests/) will make carrying out HTTP requests simpler.\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nimport requests\nfrom pyjstat import pyjstat\n\n%matplotlib inline  # allows plots to be rendered inline\n\nBPSTAT_API_URL=\"https://bpstat.bportugal.pt/data/v1\"\n```\n\nFirst, you need to grab the `series_id` for the series you're interested in. If the series page is at https://bpstat.bportugal.pt/serie/12518356, then the `series_id` is `12518356`.\n\nThe full series information can be obtained as follows:\n```python\nseries_id = 12518356\nurl = f\"{BPSTAT_API_URL}/series/?lang=EN&series_ids={series_id}\"\nseries_info = requests.get(url).json()[0]\n```\n\nNext, you need the `domain_id` and `dataset_id` from the series data you just fetched. A series can be in multiple domains but that's an exception, not the rule. If unsure, simply use the first one.\n```python\ndomain_id = series_info[\"domain_ids\"][0]\ndataset_id = series_info[\"dataset_id\"]\n```\n\nYou're now ready to request the series' observations (values) in [JSON-stat](https://json-stat.org/) format and convert them to a regular [pandas](https://pandas.pydata.org/) dataframe.\n```python\ndataset_url = f\"{BPSTAT_API_URL}/domains/{domain_id}/datasets/{dataset_id}/?lang=EN&series_ids={series_id}\"\ndataset = pyjstat.Dataset.read(dataset_url)\ndf = dataset.write('dataframe')\n```\n\nFinally, plot the values:\n```python\ndf[[\"Date\", \"value\"]].plot(kind='line', x='Date', y='value', figsize=(16, 10), marker='o')\n```\n\nAt this point, you should see a chart like this:\n![Series chart](/api/static/series_chart.png \"Series chart\")\n\nThis was a very simple example with just one series. You can extend it by fetching multiple series separately or, if they belong to the same dataset, fetch them all at once. Take a look at the documentation of each individual endpoint for more information.\n\n# Request Limits\n\nRequests to the API are rate limited. The exact mechanism used is not publicly specified and may change over time but, when limits are exceeded, requests are guaranteed to be answered with HTTP status `429`. Upon receiving this error code, clients should wait a few minutes before performing another request.\n\nNormal API responses contain an `X-Throttle` HTTP header that can be monitored to avoid error `429` responses. This header contains a **non-negative integer** that signals how close you are to hitting the request limits: further requests will be rejected for a short period of time once this value reaches zero.\n\nThe value in the `X-Throttle` header may decrease faster or slower depending on the type of request and the number of requests performed in parallel. In general, making requests that retrieve smaller amounts of data will allow you to perform more requests in a given amount of time.\n", "version": "v1"}, "host": "bpstat.bportugal.pt", "schemes": ["https"], "basePath": "/", "consumes": ["application/json"], "produces": ["application/json"], "paths": {"/data/v1/domains/": {"get": {"operationId": "domains", "summary": "List Domains", "description": "Returns a list of all available **statistical domains** (topics), in no particular order.\n\nStatistical domains form a tree hierarchy connected by the `parent_id` attribute and ordered at\neach tree level by the `order` attribute. Domains with the `has_series` attribute set to `false` don't\ncontain any datasets and are used just for structure.\n\nDomains with the `has_series` attribute set to `true` can have their `domain_id` used in other API\ncalls to obtain additional metadata or observation datasets.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```\n$ curl -sSL -X GET 'https://bpstat.bportugal.pt/data/v1/domains/?lang=EN'\n```", "parameters": [{"name": "lang", "in": "query", "description": "Language for domain labels and descriptions, one of: \n* `PT` &mdash; portuguese\n* `EN` &mdash; english", "type": "string", "default": "PT"}], "responses": {"200": {"description": "", "schema": {"type": "array", "items": {"$ref": "#/definitions/Domain"}}}}, "tags": ["API Methods"]}, "parameters": []}, "/data/v1/domains/{domain_id}/": {"get": {"operationId": "domain_detail", "summary": "Get Domain Detail", "description": "Returns information about the specified **statistical domain** (topic).\n\nStatistical domains form a tree hierarchy connected by the `parent_id` attribute and ordered at\neach tree level by the `order` attribute. If the domain has its `has_series` attribute set to `false`,\nthen it doesn't contain any datasets and is used just for structure.\n\nIf the `has_series` attribute is set to `true`, then the `domain_id` can be used in other API calls to\nobtain additional metadata or observation datasets for this domain.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```\n$ curl -sSL -X GET 'https://bpstat.bportugal.pt/data/v1/domains/59/?lang=EN'\n```", "parameters": [{"name": "domain_id", "in": "path", "description": "Domain ID.", "type": "integer", "required": true}, {"name": "lang", "in": "query", "description": "Language for domain labels and descriptions, one of: \n* `PT` &mdash; portuguese\n* `EN` &mdash; english", "type": "string", "default": "PT"}], "responses": {"200": {"description": "", "schema": {"$ref": "#/definitions/Domain"}}}, "tags": ["API Methods"]}, "parameters": [{"name": "domain_id", "in": "path", "required": true, "type": "string"}]}, "/data/v1/domains/{domain_id}/datasets/": {"get": {"operationId": "domain_datasets", "summary": "List Domain Datasets", "description": "Lists all available **datasets** in the specified statistical domain.\n\n> In this context a _dataset_ is defined as the full set of statistical series from the\n> specified domain sharing the exact same set of dimensions (across all its categories/members).\n\nThe dataset IDs can be found inside the `extension` dictionary of each collection `item`, and may\nbe used to retrieve (perhaps filtered) observations using the [Get Dataset](#operation/domain_dataset_detail)\noperation.\n\nAs the number of datasets may be large, the response may be divided into several pages.\nThe next page can be obtained by specifying the `page=n` query string parameter\n(where _n_ is the page number,